# SYNOPSIS

```usage
vx acpi import [name]
vx acpi fetch-qemu
vx acpi list
```

# DESCRIPTION

`vx acpi` keeps a corpus of ACPI tables outside the tree, in `.cache/acpi`, one directory a machine.
Firmware tables are vendors' binaries and QEMU's are GPL-licensed, so neither is committed.
The tests of the AML interpreter read the corpus.

: `import` [<name>]
  Imports this machine's tables into `.cache/acpi/<name>`, <name> being the host name by default.
  It reads `/sys/firmware/acpi/tables`, which only root can read: run it with `sudo`, and it gives what it wrote back to the user who ran `sudo`.
  It stores each table as a file named by its signature; the SSDTs that firmware methods loaded at run time (`dynamic/`) as `dynamic-<table>`; a snapshot of the firmware memory the tables' operation regions read, taken from `/dev/mem` only in ranges that `/proc/iomem` says are firmware memory (Reserved, ACPI Non-volatile Storage or ACPI Tables) with no device inside, so reading has no side effects; `linux-devices.tsv`, Linux's view of the namespace from `/sys/bus/acpi/devices` (path, hid, uid, adr, status and modalias), which the interpreter's enumeration is checked against; and a `PROVENANCE` file.
  It ends with `vx acpi: <n> tables, <n> memory regions and <n> devices in <dir>`.
: `fetch-qemu`
  Fetches the tables QEMU keeps as test data (`tests/data/acpi`) for the x86 machines q35, pc and microvm, and for arm64 and riscv64 virt, from a sparse clone of QEMU's repository at a pinned release, into `.cache/acpi/qemu-x86-q35`, `qemu-x86-pc`, `qemu-x86-microvm`, `qemu-aarch64-virt` and `qemu-riscv64-virt`.
  It refuses a release whose commit is not the pinned one.
  It needs the network.
: `list`
  Prints a line for each machine in the corpus: its count of DSDTs and SSDTs and their AML in KiB, its other tables, and whether it has Linux's device view.
  An empty corpus says so.

# FILES

: `.cache/acpi/<machine>/`
  The tables of one machine, with `PROVENANCE`.
: `/sys/firmware/acpi/tables`
  Where `import` reads.

